  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe monsoon has decided to give me a brief break tonight, as the \nheaviest showers are either in Montana or across Colorado. There \nare still a few showers around as I write this, but everything, \nat least at the time of publication is on the light side. The \nmain show still looks to be today though, especially this\nafternoon and into tonight.\n\nThe big bowling ball, AKA the Upper Level low continues to spin and \nslowly move eastward. Getting close to the Wyoming border around \nsunset. This will lead to another day of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the area. One difference is that the most numerous will \nlikely shift to the East of the Divide, especially after sunset as \nthe low moves eastward and drier air moves in behind the low. Now \nfor the main concern, which we have been talking about for the past \nseveral days, the potential for heavy rain and flooding. It will \nstill be present today, although the main threat will again be \nacross northern Wyoming. Precipitable waters one most guidance \ntop out at around an inch across the Big Horn Basin and Johnson \nCounty, approaching twice of climatological normals, then \ngradually decreasing as you head south and west. There two areas\nin particular that are concerning, the Cody Foothills and \nAbsarokas as well as Johnson County and the eastern slopes of \nthe Bighorn Range. The main concern is if the low can take a \nfurther south track, this would set up an upslope flow in these \nareas, bringing greater precipitation efficiency. The main time \nof concern would be this afternoon and evening in the Cody \nFoothills, and later tonight and possibly into Sunday morning \nfor the Bighorns and Johnson County as the low makes it's \nclosest approach. The Weather Prediction Center agrees as well, \nas the Excessive Rainfall Outlook has been upgraded to slight \nroughly north of a Cowley to Sussex line, with Marginal north of\na Lake Yellowstone to Casper Mountain line. The chance of \nflooding is less elsewhere but not zero. As for stronger \nthunderstorms, the best instability will be east of our area. \nHowever, I could see some hail with low wet bulb zero levels at \ntimes. Precipitation should finally end in western Wyoming later\ntonight as the low moves eastward.\n\nThere is one more day to get through on Sunday. The low will be \nstill be moving eastward, finally crossing into South Dakota and \nNebraska Sunday night. Some wrap around moisture will still be \naround in northern Wyoming though, especially along and east of the \nBighorns. The threat of heavy rain will likely linger another day \nhere, especially in the morning before gradually tapering off in the \nafternoon. Chances of showers decrease further west, with most \nof western and southwestern Wyoming being dry by noon on Sunday,\nAs for the flood watch, we will continue it as is this morning.\nWe may be able to trim some of the western zones out for Sunday\nif the low moves far enough east, but we will cross that bridge\nwhen we get there.\n\nRidging builds over the area on Monday, bringing a nice last full \nday of astronomical summer with mild temperatures and a good deal of \nsunshine for most locations. Models are still split from later \nTuesday through Wednesday with the next potential system in the\nform of a trough moving in from the southwest. Some guidance, \nlike the European, brings it across the state bringing another \nchance of showers. Other guidance keeps it to the south with \nmainly dry conditions. The current forecast leans toward the \nwetter solution, but this is far from certain. Mainly dry \nweather then likely returns for Thursday and Friday. And, with \nlargely south to southwest flow continuing, temperatures will \nlikely average above normal through much of next week as we head\ninto astronomical autumn, which begins Tuesday at 6:04 pm.",
